# A borax snowflake: why some things crystallise on cooling and some do not
#
# Borax is the reason this experiment is done with borax. Cold water holds
# almost none of it; boiling water holds eleven times as much. Dissolve all
# you can in hot water, let it cool, and the water has to give most of it
# back — as crystals.
register lv2

# 1. Cold water first, so you can see how little it takes. Twenty-five
#    grams goes in and most of it just sits there.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 Na2B4O7 25g
inspect v1

# 2. Heat it. Now the same water swallows almost all of it.
heat v1 25kJ
inspect v1

# 3. And cool it right down. What the water can no longer hold comes back
#    out — 0.096 mol of it, which is most of what dissolved.
cool v1 30kJ
inspect v1

# 4. The contrast that makes the point, and the bench draws it sharper than
#    the numbers alone do. Sugar's solubility also rises with temperature —
#    200 to 487 g per 100 mL, a bit over double, against borax's 2.5 to
#    27.4, eleven times. But watch what the two do on the way down: the
#    borax comes OUT, and the sugar syrup goes SUPERSATURATED and stays
#    dissolved at 1.25 times what the water should hold. That is the same
#    difference a kitchen sees. A borax snowflake grows overnight on its
#    own; rock candy sits there refusing to start until you give it a
#    string to grow on.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
add v2 sucrose 250g
heat v2 25kJ
cool v2 30kJ
inspect v2

# What this does not model: the crystal. There is no pipe cleaner to grow
# on, no habit, no six-fold symmetry — the bench says how much solid comes
# back, not what shape it takes, and a snowflake is entirely about shape.
#
# And a stated substitution: handbook solubilities for borax are for the
# decahydrate, Na2B4O7·10H2O, which is also what really crystallises out of
# a cooling solution. This registry has only the anhydrous salt, so it
# stands in for the decahydrate at the decahydrate's solubility, scaled by
# the mass ratio. The amounts are right; the solid that carries them is a
# stand-in.
